# Shell scripts must keep LF line endings on every platform. On Windows,
# checkout would otherwise convert them to CRLF, which breaks the shebang under
# Git Bash and makes shellcheck fail with SC1017 (literal carriage return).
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.zsh   text eol=lf
*.fish  text eol=lf
*.bats  text eol=lf
bin/*   text eol=lf
# bin/* does not recurse; the private verb implementations live one level down
# and are extensionless, so they need their own rule to stay LF (a CRLF shebang
# breaks them under sh, e.g. in the Linux test container).
bin/git-review-verbs/* text eol=lf
install.sh   text eol=lf
uninstall.sh text eol=lf

# Both containers are built from a Windows checkout as often as from a Linux
# one, and `core.autocrlf=true` would hand the build a CRLF Dockerfile — where a
# trailing backslash is followed by a carriage return and the line continuation
# is no longer one. Extensionless, so *.sh above does not cover them.
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# VERSION travels in the release tarball and is read verbatim by the version
# tests; a CRLF here breaks the bare-semver check and every version comparison.
VERSION text eol=lf

# shellcheck reads .shellcheckrc as a script and rejects CR (SC1017), so it must
# stay LF on Windows checkouts too.
.shellcheckrc text eol=lf

# A walkthrough is authored on one machine and read on every reviewer's, so its
# line endings must not depend on who committed it. The readers tolerate CRLF
# (walk_strip_cr), but keeping the committed bytes LF keeps the sidecar diffable
# across platforms. This rule only governs *this* repo dogfooding git review on
# itself; a consumer repo's walkthrough is covered by the reader-side strip.
.review/*.md text eol=lf

# pluginVersion in gradle.properties is the JetBrains plugin's only source of
# truth and the version tests read it verbatim to the end of the line, so a CRLF
# checkout makes the bare-semver check see "0.2.0\r" and fail. Same reasoning as
# VERSION above; Gradle itself is happy either way, the tests are not. Without
# this the documented Windows dev loop (bats in the Linux container over the
# Windows working tree) fails on a repo that is green in CI.
jetbrains-plugin/gradle.properties text eol=lf
